import{_ as a,H as r,c as n,b as i,a2 as t,K as s}from"./chunks/framework.BEqfOpL-.js";const y=JSON.parse('{"title":"Distribution Health","description":"Distribution Health gives you a complete picture of where your podcast is listed and whether each of those listings is healthy. Your RSS feed is the single file every podcast directory reads, so when it works well your episodes show up everywhere they should — and when something goes wrong, it can quietly affect every app at once. This page helps you confirm your feed is sound, see where your show is distributed, and catch problems before they cost you listeners.","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"features/distribution-health.md","filePath":"features/distribution-health.md"}'),h={name:"features/distribution-health.md"};function d(l,e,p,c,u,g){const o=r("HelpWalkthrough");return i(),n("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t('<h1 id="distribution-health" tabindex="-1">Distribution Health <a class="header-anchor" href="#distribution-health" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Distribution Health&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="make-sure-every-app-and-directory-can-find-your-show" tabindex="-1">Make Sure Every App and Directory Can Find Your Show <a class="header-anchor" href="#make-sure-every-app-and-directory-can-find-your-show" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Make Sure Every App and Directory Can Find Your Show&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Distribution Health gives you a complete picture of where your podcast is listed and whether each of those listings is healthy. Your RSS feed is the single file every podcast directory reads, so when it works well your episodes show up everywhere they should — and when something goes wrong, it can quietly affect every app at once. This page helps you confirm your feed is sound, see where your show is distributed, and catch problems before they cost you listeners.</p><p><strong>To access</strong>: in the sidebar, under <strong>Get found</strong>, select <strong>Distribution</strong>. The page has five tabs: <strong>Overview</strong>, <strong>Presence</strong>, <strong>Feed &amp; issues</strong>, <strong>Appearance</strong> and <strong>History</strong>. If you manage more than one show, <strong>View distribution for all shows</strong> opens a summary of every show.</p><h2 id="check-the-feed-then-the-audio-path" tabindex="-1">Check the feed, then the audio path <a class="header-anchor" href="#check-the-feed-then-the-audio-path" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Check the feed, then the audio path&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li>Open the <strong>Feed &amp; issues</strong> tab. In <strong>Check feed health</strong>, select <strong>Run feed check</strong>.</li><li>Read the RSS status, valid episode count, and <strong>Issues Found</strong>. Fix errors first; a Well-Formed feed can still have episode or metadata warnings.</li><li>Inspect <strong>Feed Fetch Performance</strong> for the RSS request. Read <strong>Episode Download Analysis</strong> separately for the audio redirects and buffering.</li></ol>',6)),s(o,{src:"/walkthroughs/distribution-diagnostics.webp",poster:"/images/feed_health_check_speed.webp",alt:"Run a feed check, read RSS validation findings, and distinguish feed-fetch timing from the episode audio download waterfall",duration:21650,autoplay:"",loop:"",sample:""}),e[1]||(e[1]=t('<p>The walkthrough uses sample diagnostic data. Apply feed and audio-host fixes through your podcast host, then re-run the check. RSS fetch timing and the audio waterfall measure different requests; one does not establish the other&#39;s speed.</p><hr><h2 id="overview-your-distribution-at-a-glance" tabindex="-1"><strong>Overview: Your Distribution at a Glance</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#overview-your-distribution-at-a-glance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Overview: Your Distribution at a Glance**&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Overview</strong> is the first tab. <strong>Your show across platforms</strong> shows your cover and whether PodSEO found a listing on each of the major listening apps. Below it:</p><ul><li><strong>A good next step</strong>: the one thing most worth doing now, such as reviewing your feed or adding a missing listing, with a button that takes you there.</li><li>Shortcuts to <strong>Feed &amp; issues</strong>, <strong>Appearance</strong> (how your show looks on the apps) and <strong>More ways to listen</strong> (other apps and directories).</li><li><strong>All platforms</strong> opens the <strong>Presence</strong> tab with every platform.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="presence-where-you-re-listed" tabindex="-1"><strong>Presence: Where You&#39;re Listed</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#presence-where-you-re-listed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Presence: Where You&#39;re Listed**&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Presence</strong> lists every app and directory, in sections you can jump to from the shortcuts at the top: <strong>Primary distribution</strong>, <strong>Grow your reach</strong>, <strong>App and directory links</strong>, and, when available, <strong>Share your show</strong> and <strong>Regional opportunities</strong>.</p><h3 id="primary-distribution" tabindex="-1"><strong>Primary Distribution</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#primary-distribution" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Primary Distribution**&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Primary distribution</strong> covers the four apps where most podcast listening happens: Apple Podcasts, Spotify, Amazon Music, and YouTube Music. Each platform gets a row with a status:</p><ul><li><strong>One listing found</strong>: PodSEO found your show on that platform.</li><li><strong>More than one listing</strong>: multiple listings usually mean duplicates, which split your rankings and reviews. Check them, and contact that platform&#39;s support to merge them where possible.</li><li><strong>Not detected yet</strong>: PodSEO keeps checking automatically. If your show is already on the platform, expand the row and use <strong>Add URL manually</strong> to paste its public page. If it isn&#39;t, use <strong>Submit to</strong> to open the platform&#39;s own submission page (see <a href="#how-do-i-submit-my-podcast-to-the-main-apps">How do I submit my podcast to the main apps?</a>).</li></ul><p>If a platform isn&#39;t available for your show (for example, YouTube Music RSS upload is not offered in your country), choose <strong>Mark unavailable</strong> and it is left out of your distribution score. <strong>Include in score</strong> puts it back.</p><p>If you paste your own listing and it was previously attached to an old podcast that has since been deleted from PodSEO, it&#39;s now linked to your show straight away.</p><h3 id="grow-your-reach" tabindex="-1"><strong>Grow Your Reach</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#grow-your-reach" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Grow Your Reach**&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Grow your reach</strong> lists more directories that have their own show pages: iHeartRadio, TuneIn, Deezer, Pandora, Listen Notes, Fyyd, Podcast App, and Blubrry for shows hosted on Blubrry. Search each one for your show and save the link you find. If your show isn&#39;t there, <strong>Submission details</strong> opens that directory&#39;s submission page. PodSEO keeps the links you save but doesn&#39;t independently verify or monitor them.</p><h3 id="app-and-directory-links" tabindex="-1"><strong>App and Directory Links</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#app-and-directory-links" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**App and Directory Links**&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>App and directory links</strong> gives you ready-made links to open your show in more podcast apps, such as Overcast, Pocket Casts, Castbox, Podcast Addict, Fountain and Podverse. Most of these apps list shows automatically once you&#39;re on Apple Podcasts or Podcast Index, so there is usually nothing to submit. The links are generated from your show&#39;s details, so check a link before sharing it widely. If PodSEO doesn&#39;t find your show on Podcast Index, it offers a link to add your feed there.</p><h3 id="share-your-show" tabindex="-1"><strong>Share Your Show</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#share-your-show" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Share Your Show**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>When available, <strong>Share your show</strong> lists smart links: a single URL that sends each listener to the podcast app they use. They&#39;re ideal for your social bios, email signature, and show notes.</p><h3 id="regional-opportunities" tabindex="-1"><strong>Regional Opportunities</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#regional-opportunities" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Regional Opportunities**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>When relevant, <strong>Regional opportunities</strong> suggests directories for specific languages, countries, or audiences (for example Spanish-language, Indian, Arabic or European platforms). PodSEO doesn&#39;t track these, so open them directly and submit your show if it fits their audience.</p><hr><h2 id="how-do-i-submit-my-podcast-to-the-main-apps" tabindex="-1"><strong>How Do I Submit My Podcast to the Main Apps?</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-do-i-submit-my-podcast-to-the-main-apps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**How Do I Submit My Podcast to the Main Apps?**&quot;">​</a></h2><p>PodSEO can&#39;t submit your show for you: each platform asks you to sign in and prove the show is yours. Many podcast hosts can also send your show to these apps for you from their dashboard. You need your RSS feed address, and the owner email in your feed should be one you can read, because most platforms send a confirmation code there.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>Where to submit</th><th>Good to know</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Apple Podcasts</td><td><a href="https://podcastsconnect.apple.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Apple Podcasts Connect</a> → add a show with an RSS feed</td><td>Apple reviews new shows before they go live. Many apps (Overcast, Castbox, Goodpods and others) pick up shows from Apple.</td></tr><tr><td>Spotify</td><td><a href="https://creators.spotify.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Spotify for Creators</a> (formerly Spotify for Podcasters) → find an existing show</td><td>Spotify confirms ownership with a code sent to the feed&#39;s email.</td></tr><tr><td>Amazon Music</td><td><a href="https://podcasters.amazon.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Amazon Music for Podcasters</a></td><td>One listing covers Amazon Music, Audible and Alexa. Approval can take up to a week.</td></tr><tr><td>YouTube Music</td><td>YouTube Studio → create a podcast → submit your RSS feed (<a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/13525207" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">YouTube&#39;s guide</a>)</td><td>Feeds with ads in the audio aren&#39;t accepted, and RSS upload is offered only in some countries. Google Podcasts has closed; its listeners moved to YouTube Music.</td></tr><tr><td>Podcast Index</td><td><a href="https://podcastindex.org/add" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">podcastindex.org/add</a></td><td>Feeds Fountain, Podverse and other Podcasting 2.0 apps. Many hosts add your show automatically.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Once your show is live, PodSEO finds it on its next check, or you can paste the public page with <strong>Add URL manually</strong>.</p><hr><h2 id="feed-issues" tabindex="-1"><strong>Feed &amp; Issues</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#feed-issues" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Feed &amp; Issues**&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <strong>Feed &amp; issues</strong> tab is where you check that your feed works.</p><h3 id="last-feed-response" tabindex="-1"><strong>Last Feed Response</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#last-feed-response" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Last Feed Response**&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Last feed response</strong> shows the last answer PodSEO recorded from your feed (for example HTTP 200). A good response confirms the feed answered, not that its content is valid. Below it, the <a href="#feed-document-audit">Feed document audit</a> and the live <a href="#feed-analysis">feed check</a> look at the content.</p><p>If PodSEO finds more than one listing on a platform, a <strong>Review multiple listings</strong> note links to them.</p><h3 id="feed" tabindex="-1"><strong>Feed</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#feed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Feed**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Your <strong>Feed</strong> is the RSS file every directory pulls from. PodSEO shows your primary feed along with any alternate or redirected feeds it detects, each with a status indicator. Having a single, clean primary feed matters: alternate or duplicate feeds can split your stats and confuse directories about which version to follow.</p><p>Under the feed, a freshness panel shows when PodSEO last read your feed and what it currently has on file: <strong>Title</strong>, <strong>Author</strong>, <strong>Description</strong>, <strong>Episodes</strong>, and <strong>Latest episode</strong>. If you just changed something at your host and don&#39;t see it reflected yet, click <strong>Read it again</strong> to ask PodSEO to re-read your feed. The new values appear within a few minutes.</p><p>If you upload episodes directly in Apple Podcasts Connect or Spotify for Creators, your show may have no RSS feed: that&#39;s normal, and the page says so.</p><h3 id="website" tabindex="-1"><strong>Website</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#website" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Website**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Your <strong>Website</strong> is your show&#39;s home on the web, detected from your RSS feed. A dedicated website is one of the biggest discoverability levers you control, because it&#39;s the only place your show gets Google-indexed pages and a custom domain that no platform can lock away. If PodSEO doesn&#39;t find a custom website, it flags that here.</p><hr><h2 id="appearance-how-your-show-looks-on-the-apps" tabindex="-1"><strong>Appearance: How Your Show Looks on the Apps</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#appearance-how-your-show-looks-on-the-apps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Appearance: How Your Show Looks on the Apps**&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <strong>Appearance</strong> tab shows how each app presents your show, with a switch for <strong>Apple Podcasts</strong> and <strong>Spotify</strong>: Apple first, then <a href="#how-you-appear-on-spotify">Spotify</a>.</p><h2 id="how-you-appear-on-apple-podcasts" tabindex="-1"><strong>How You Appear on Apple Podcasts</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-you-appear-on-apple-podcasts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**How You Appear on Apple Podcasts**&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Your feed decides <strong>whether</strong> Apple can list your show. This section shows <strong>how</strong> Apple can present it, next to your competitors. Beyond the square cover, Apple uses wide and tall artwork for the shows it features: a large banner at the top of the show page on Apple TV and Mac, a full-page image on iPhone and iPad, and banners in its editorial collections. Many podcasters don&#39;t know this artwork exists, so the card shows it with the real images.</p><p>Open <strong>Appearance</strong> and choose <strong>Apple Podcasts</strong>.</p><h3 id="first-step-claim-your-show-on-apple-podcasts-connect" tabindex="-1"><strong>First Step: Claim Your Show on Apple Podcasts Connect</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#first-step-claim-your-show-on-apple-podcasts-connect" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**First Step: Claim Your Show on Apple Podcasts Connect**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>If you&#39;ve never claimed your show on <a href="https://podcastsconnect.apple.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Apple Podcasts Connect</a>, start there. It&#39;s where you prove the show is yours, manage your Apple listing and see your Apple analytics. The window links to it (<strong>Haven&#39;t claimed your show? Start with Apple Podcasts Connect</strong>); the link is hidden once your Apple Podcasts Connect account is connected to PodSEO in <a href="/features/analytics-connections.html">Analytics Connections</a>. For tips on requesting a feature, see Apple&#39;s guide to <a href="https://podcasters.apple.com/support/1993-optimize-your-request-for-promotion" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">optimizing your request for promotion</a>. What claiming unlocks, on Apple and Spotify: <a href="/academy/why-you-should-claim-your-podcast-on-apple-and-spotify.html">Why You Should Claim Your Podcast on Apple Podcasts and Spotify</a>.</p><h3 id="what-does-the-your-show-on-apple-podcasts-card-show" tabindex="-1"><strong>What Does the &quot;Your Show on Apple Podcasts&quot; Card Show?</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-does-the-your-show-on-apple-podcasts-card-show" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**What Does the &quot;Your Show on Apple Podcasts&quot; Card Show?**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The card puts your square cover next to a wide preview of the Apple TV and Mac artwork. If Apple has that artwork for your show, you see yours. If not, PodSEO shows one of your competitors&#39; as an example (labelled with its name), or an outline of the format when none of them has it. Below, a short summary says how many editorial images Apple returned for your show and how many of your checked competitors have editorial artwork, plus the date of the last check.</p><p>Click <strong>Explore artwork and compare</strong> to open the full view.</p><h3 id="the-explore-artwork-and-compare-window" tabindex="-1"><strong>The &quot;Explore Artwork and Compare&quot; Window</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-explore-artwork-and-compare-window" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**The &quot;Explore Artwork and Compare&quot; Window**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The window, titled <strong>How Apple can show your podcast</strong>, is a gallery of previews built from the artwork Apple returns for a listing. Each frame is one place where Apple can use artwork:</p><ul><li><strong>Apple TV and Mac</strong> — the wide 16:9 artwork Apple can place at the top of the show page on Apple TV and Mac</li><li><strong>Content logo</strong> — the white logo Apple can lay over the wide artwork</li><li><strong>Show page</strong> — the tall artwork Apple can show behind the show page on iPhone and iPad (Apple&#39;s size: 2048 × 2732 px)</li><li><strong>Editorial banner</strong> — the 4:1 banner Apple&#39;s editors can use when they feature the show</li><li><strong>Subscription banner</strong> — the 4:1 banner Apple can use to promote a show&#39;s subscription</li><li><strong>More artwork variants</strong> — a wide 4:3 backdrop, a 2:3 portrait cover and a tall 3:4 backdrop, behind a disclosure</li></ul><p>Under the gallery, <strong>Listing basics</strong> shows your cover as Apple has it, with the size Apple reports, and the categories Apple shows for the show.</p><p>These are previews of possible placements, not screenshots of what Apple is showing today. Having this artwork doesn&#39;t mean Apple is promoting the show right now, and submitting it doesn&#39;t guarantee a placement: <strong>Apple decides what it features</strong>.</p><h3 id="what-does-not-returned-by-apple-mean" tabindex="-1"><strong>What Does &quot;Not Returned by Apple&quot; Mean?</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-does-not-returned-by-apple-mean" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**What Does &quot;Not Returned by Apple&quot; Mean?**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>When Apple has no artwork for a placement, the frame stays empty, shows the size Apple asks for, and reads <strong>Not returned by Apple</strong>. It means Apple didn&#39;t return that artwork for the listing. It isn&#39;t an error and it doesn&#39;t affect your health score or any other PodSEO score. Most shows have only the square cover: Apple usually has editorial artwork for shows it has chosen to feature.</p><p>If a preview reads <strong>Preview unavailable</strong>, the image couldn&#39;t be loaded this time; it doesn&#39;t mean the artwork is missing.</p><h3 id="how-do-i-compare-my-apple-listing-with-competitors" tabindex="-1"><strong>How Do I Compare My Apple Listing With Competitors?</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-do-i-compare-my-apple-listing-with-competitors" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**How Do I Compare My Apple Listing With Competitors?**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Use the <strong>Showing</strong> menu at the top of the window to switch between <strong>Your show</strong> and each of your competitors. The gallery keeps the same order, so you can compare placement by placement. Under each main frame (not the ones under <strong>More artwork variants</strong>), <strong>Also has this</strong> shows small thumbnails of the other shows that have that artwork; click one to switch the gallery to that show. When none of them has it, the frame reads <strong>No other checked listing has this</strong>.</p><p>If you haven&#39;t added competitors yet, use <strong>Add competitors to compare</strong>. If none of the checked shows has editorial artwork, the window says so: that&#39;s common in many niches and not something you did wrong.</p><h3 id="how-do-i-fix-my-cover-or-categories-on-apple-podcasts" tabindex="-1"><strong>How Do I Fix My Cover or Categories on Apple Podcasts?</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-do-i-fix-my-cover-or-categories-on-apple-podcasts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**How Do I Fix My Cover or Categories on Apple Podcasts?**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Your cover and categories come from your RSS feed, so change them in your podcast hosting platform:</p><ul><li><strong>Cover art</strong> — Apple asks for a square image between 1,400 and 3,000 px. If yours is smaller, the window flags it with <strong>Below the 1,400 px Apple asks for</strong>.</li><li><strong>Categories</strong> — choose the categories that describe your show in your host&#39;s settings. If Apple only shows the generic &quot;Podcasts&quot;, your show has no specific category on Apple.</li></ul><p>Apple picks up the change the next time it reads your feed; PodSEO shows it after its next check.</p><h3 id="how-do-i-get-the-new-apple-tv-and-mac-artwork" tabindex="-1"><strong>How Do I Get the New Apple TV and Mac Artwork?</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-do-i-get-the-new-apple-tv-and-mac-artwork" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**How Do I Get the New Apple TV and Mac Artwork?**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The wide Apple TV and Mac artwork and the content logo are by invitation: Apple emails the shows it invites and asks for two files.</p><ul><li><strong>Universal Art</strong> — 16:9, 5244 × 2950 px, as a PSD and a PNG, with no text or logos in the image</li><li><strong>Content Logo</strong> — white, 4320 × 1300 px, on a transparent background</li><li>Both in the <strong>Display P3</strong> color space, <strong>16-bit</strong></li></ul><p>Upload them through the <strong>Box link in Apple&#39;s email</strong>. If you haven&#39;t received that email, Apple hasn&#39;t invited your show yet. Inside the window, <strong>How to provide artwork</strong> summarizes the same steps.</p><h3 id="network-and-apple-podcasts-subscriptions" tabindex="-1"><strong>Network and Apple Podcasts Subscriptions</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#network-and-apple-podcasts-subscriptions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Network and Apple Podcasts Subscriptions**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The <strong>Network and subscriptions on Apple</strong> card shows whether Apple lists your show under a network (an Apple channel). If it does, you see the network&#39;s name and how many shows Apple lists on it, with a link to the channel page on Apple Podcasts. Most shows are not part of a network, and the card then reads <strong>Not part of an Apple network</strong>: there&#39;s nothing to fix.</p><p><strong>Apple Podcasts Subscriptions</strong> says whether Apple sells paid subscriptions for your show (<strong>Offered</strong> or <strong>Not offered</strong>). An <strong>Apple+</strong> label means the show is also part of Apple&#39;s Apple+ offer. Until PodSEO has read these details from Apple, both lines say <strong>Not checked yet</strong>: that means &quot;unknown&quot;, not &quot;no&quot;.</p><p>In <strong>Competitive Analysis</strong> and <strong>Competitor Finder</strong>, a small <strong>Network</strong> label marks competitors that Apple lists under a network (hover it for the name), and a <strong>Subscriptions</strong> label marks competitors that sell Apple Podcasts Subscriptions.</p><h3 id="how-often-is-my-apple-listing-checked" tabindex="-1"><strong>How Often Is My Apple Listing Checked?</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-often-is-my-apple-listing-checked" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**How Often Is My Apple Listing Checked?**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>PodSEO checks your show and your competitors on Apple Podcasts once a week, and right after you add a show or a competitor. The card and the window show the date of the last check. A show reads <strong>Not checked yet</strong> until its first check, and <strong>No Apple Podcasts listing linked</strong> when PodSEO doesn&#39;t know its Apple listing: add it in <strong>Presence</strong> → <strong>Primary distribution</strong> with <strong>Add URL manually</strong>.</p><hr><h2 id="how-you-appear-on-spotify" tabindex="-1"><strong>How You Appear on Spotify</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-you-appear-on-spotify" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**How You Appear on Spotify**&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Appearance → Spotify</strong> shows your Spotify listing as PodSEO last read it. Under the cover, <strong>Trailer</strong> says whether Spotify shows a trailer on your show page (and links to it), and <strong>Spotify subscription: offered</strong> appears when listeners can subscribe to your show on Spotify. <strong>Not checked yet</strong> means PodSEO hasn&#39;t read that detail yet, not that it&#39;s missing. The episode list shows, for each episode, the release date, the length, whether Spotify plays it as <strong>Audio</strong> or <strong>Video</strong>, a <strong>Paid</strong> tag for paywalled episodes and the wide 16:9 cover when there is one. PodSEO stores the latest 50 episodes Spotify lists at each check, starting November 12, 2026; until your show&#39;s first check the page says the details are being collected. If your feed marks a trailer episode but Spotify shows none, PodSEO&#39;s health check suggests setting it in <a href="https://creators.spotify.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Spotify for Creators</a>: open the trailer episode there and choose it as your show&#39;s trailer.</p><h3 id="do-my-competitors-publish-video-on-spotify" tabindex="-1"><strong>Do My Competitors Publish Video on Spotify?</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#do-my-competitors-publish-video-on-spotify" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Do My Competitors Publish Video on Spotify?**&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Video on Spotify</strong>, under <strong>Ways to make your show stand out</strong>, compares your show with the competitors you selected: for each show, the share of its latest 20 Spotify episodes that Spotify plays as video (for example <strong>75% video</strong>, 15 of 20 episodes). PodSEO reads these details only for shows it checks on Spotify, so a show reads <strong>Not checked yet</strong> until its first check, never 0%. When at least two of your competitors are checked, most of them publish video and your latest episodes are audio only, LUCIA may suggest publishing your next episode as video too.</p><hr><h2 id="feed-analysis" tabindex="-1"><strong>Feed Analysis</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#feed-analysis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Feed Analysis**&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On the <strong>Feed &amp; issues</strong> tab, find <strong>Check feed health</strong> and select <strong>Run feed check</strong> to run a full read of your RSS feed. Run it again whenever you want to re-test your feed. The analysis validates how your feed is built and surfaces anything that could trip up the apps that rely on it.</p><h3 id="feed-document-audit" tabindex="-1"><strong>Feed Document Audit</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#feed-document-audit" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Feed Document Audit**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Above the live check, the <strong>Feed document audit</strong> card shows what your last health report found in your feed. Besides structural problems, it can point out:</p><ul><li><strong>Hidden from Apple Podcasts</strong>: your feed sets <code>itunes:block</code> to yes, which tells Apple to remove the show. Turn the &quot;block&quot; or &quot;hide from Apple&quot; option off in your host&#39;s settings if you didn&#39;t mean it.</li><li><strong>Patreon without a support link</strong>: your episode notes link to Patreon but the feed has no <code>podcast:funding</code> tag, so apps that show a support button can&#39;t show yours.</li><li><strong>Is this your YouTube channel?</strong>: your notes keep linking a YouTube channel and PodSEO has none for your show. Click <strong>This is my channel</strong> only if it&#39;s your show&#39;s own channel (notes often link to guests&#39; channels, so PodSEO never connects one by itself); your YouTube rankings appear after the next rank check.</li><li><strong>Apple Podcasts and Spotify checks</strong> (when available for your report): episodes from your feed that Apple Podcasts still doesn&#39;t list after two days, and under <strong>Worth knowing</strong>, episodes Apple sells to subscribers only and how many letters of your show&#39;s name listeners type before Spotify&#39;s search box suggests it.</li></ul><h3 id="feed-status-and-health-checklist" tabindex="-1"><strong>Feed Status and Health Checklist</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#feed-status-and-health-checklist" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Feed Status and Health Checklist**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The analysis leads with a clear <strong>Well-Formed</strong> or <strong>Malformed</strong> status, then a short pass/fail checklist covering the essentials:</p><ul><li><strong>Well-formed XML</strong> — Confirms your feed is structured correctly so directories can read it</li><li><strong>Valid Episodes</strong> — How many of your episodes are valid out of the total found in the feed</li><li><strong>Podcast 2.0 Tags</strong> — Whether your feed uses the newer Podcast 2.0 tags that unlock richer features in modern apps</li><li><strong>Feed Size</strong> — The overall size of your feed file</li></ul><p>You&#39;ll also see a quick <strong>Feed Statistics</strong> summary of your total and valid episode counts, and — when present — the specific Podcast 2.0 features your feed includes.</p><h3 id="if-the-result-says-malformed" tabindex="-1">If the result says Malformed <a class="header-anchor" href="#if-the-result-says-malformed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;If the result says Malformed&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A <strong>Malformed</strong> result means PodSEO could not parse the RSS XML. Read the error under <strong>Issues Found</strong> and take it to your podcast host; a new check cannot fix the feed by itself. The separate example below shows a malformed sample feed, rather than the valid feed in the first walkthrough.</p>',91)),s(o,{src:"/walkthroughs/distribution-feed-errors.webp",poster:"/images/feed_health_check_speed.webp",alt:"Feed-check overview; play the walkthrough to inspect a separate malformed XML example and its parsing error",duration:15250,autoplay:"",loop:"",sample:""}),e[2]||(e[2]=t('<h3 id="issues-found" tabindex="-1"><strong>Issues Found</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#issues-found" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Issues Found**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Rather than reducing everything to a single number, Distribution Health tells you exactly what needs attention by listing the specific <strong>Issues Found</strong> in your feed, grouped into:</p><ul><li><strong>Errors</strong> — Problems that can prevent your feed from being read or distributed correctly, which should be fixed first</li><li><strong>Warnings</strong> — Less urgent issues worth reviewing and cleaning up over time</li></ul><p>Each entry describes the issue directly so you know what to take to your podcast host. If your feed is clean, you simply won&#39;t see any errors or warnings — that&#39;s a good sign.</p><hr><h2 id="feed-fetch-performance" tabindex="-1"><strong>Feed Fetch Performance</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#feed-fetch-performance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Feed Fetch Performance**&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A core part of this page is the <strong>Feed Fetch Performance</strong> panel, which measures how quickly your feed actually loads — and what it has to go through to get there.</p><p>Every analytics tracker or ad-insertion service you add to your feed creates a redirect — an extra hop between a directory requesting your feed and actually receiving it. Stacking multiple redirects slows things down, inflates your feed size, and can cause failures on some platforms.</p><p>This panel shows:</p><ul><li><strong>Initial and final URL</strong> — Where the request starts and where it ends up after any redirects</li><li><strong>Redirect chain</strong> — Each hop in the chain, with its response status and the time that hop took, so you can see exactly where delay is being introduced</li><li><strong>Total time</strong> — How long the whole fetch took from start to finish</li><li><strong>Feed size</strong> — Your feed file size, graded so you can tell at a glance whether it&#39;s a good size, getting large, or too large</li><li><strong>Redirects</strong> — The total number of hops in the chain</li></ul><p>If you see a long redirect chain or slow total time, review which tracking services are still necessary and remove any you no longer use. For a deeper look at why this matters, see our academy article on <a href="/academy/why-multiple-podcast-trackers-are-a-recipe-for-disaster.html">why multiple podcast trackers are a recipe for disaster</a>.</p><hr><h2 id="history-recorded-checks-and-changes" tabindex="-1"><strong>History: Recorded Checks and Changes</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#history-recorded-checks-and-changes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**History: Recorded Checks and Changes**&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>History</strong> lists what PodSEO recorded about your show over time: changes to your Apple and Spotify listings (title, cover, trailer, subscription) and your saved feed reports, each with <strong>Before</strong> and <strong>After</strong> values. Dates show when PodSEO recorded the information, which may differ from when it actually changed. History starts with the first saved data.</p><hr><h2 id="after-the-diagnostic" tabindex="-1">After the diagnostic <a class="header-anchor" href="#after-the-diagnostic" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;After the diagnostic&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Confirm that the platform listings belong to your show. Add missing listings with <strong>Add URL manually</strong>, or submit the show with <strong>Submit to</strong>, and ask the platform to resolve duplicates where necessary. Use <strong>Explore artwork and compare</strong> for Apple&#39;s artwork previews; those images do not indicate that Apple is featuring you now.</p><hr><h2 id="best-practices-for-distribution-health" tabindex="-1"><strong>Best Practices for Distribution Health</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#best-practices-for-distribution-health" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Best Practices for Distribution Health**&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="check-after-changes" tabindex="-1"><strong>Check After Changes</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#check-after-changes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Check After Changes**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Revisit Distribution Health after you change your hosting setup, update metadata, or add or remove a tracker. This is the fastest way to confirm a change had the effect you intended and didn&#39;t introduce a new issue.</p><h3 id="keep-your-redirect-chain-short" tabindex="-1"><strong>Keep Your Redirect Chain Short</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#keep-your-redirect-chain-short" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Keep Your Redirect Chain Short**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Each tracker you stack onto your feed adds time and size. Periodically prune services you no longer use — a shorter chain means faster loads and fewer chances for playback failures.</p><h3 id="consolidate-to-a-single-clean-feed" tabindex="-1"><strong>Consolidate to a Single Clean Feed</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#consolidate-to-a-single-clean-feed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Consolidate to a Single Clean Feed**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Multiple active or duplicate feeds can split your stats and send directories to the wrong place. Aim for one clear primary feed that every app follows.</p><h3 id="claim-your-own-website" tabindex="-1"><strong>Claim Your Own Website</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#claim-your-own-website" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Claim Your Own Website**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>If Distribution Health flags a missing custom website, it&#39;s worth acting on. A dedicated site gives your show indexed pages and a presence that doesn&#39;t depend on any single platform.</p><hr><h2 id="working-with-other-podseo-features" tabindex="-1"><strong>Working with Other PodSEO Features</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#working-with-other-podseo-features" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**Working with Other PodSEO Features**&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A healthy, fast-loading feed supports everything else you do in PodSEO. Clean metadata strengthens the keyword work you do in <a href="/features/keyword-strategy.html">Keyword Strategy</a>, and reliable distribution underpins the discoverability you track in <a href="/features/visibility-progress.html">Visibility Progress</a>. You can also see a quick distribution summary on the <a href="/features/podcast-details.html">Overview</a> tab, with a link straight back into this tab when you want the full diagnostic.</p><hr><p>Open <strong>Distribution → Feed &amp; issues</strong>, select <strong>Run feed check</strong>, and review your feed status, issues, and fetch performance. Use the issues listed there as a checklist when you make changes through your podcast host.</p>',32))])}const w=a(h,[["render",d]]);export{y as __pageData,w as default};
